`pulse.frag` currently switches abruptly between black and white every half-second. Replace it with a cosine pulse: black → white → black over one second, repeating smoothly.

```glsl
uniform float uTime; // Elapsed seconds, supplied by the host.

void main() {
    float phase = fract(uTime);
    float pulse = 0.5 - 0.5 * cos(6.28318530718 * phase);
    gl_FragColor = vec4(vec3(pulse), 1.0);
}
```

Keep updating `uTime` from the existing host loop. The pulse reaches 0 at whole seconds, 1 at half-seconds, and has zero slope at both extrema.

`lights.js` currently provides only uniform ambient illumination. This replacement adds a stronger warm key and a weaker cool fill, aimed at the subject’s world-space center:

```js
export function illuminate(THREE, scene, center = [0, 0, 0]) {
  const key = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xfff2df, 2.0);
  key.position.set(center[0] + 5, center[1] + 6, center[2] + 5);
  key.target.position.set(...center);

  const fill = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xddeaff, 0.6);
  fill.position.set(center[0] - 5, center[1] + 2, center[2] + 3);
  fill.target.position.set(...center);

  scene.add(key, key.target, fill, fill.target);

  return { key, fill };
}
```

This assumes Y-up and the subject’s front toward +Z. The roughly 3.3:1 intensity ratio gives directional shape while lifting the darker side. Use it in place of the old ambient setup; any existing ambient instance must be removed by its owner. Callers now receive `{ key, fill }` instead of one light.

The host retains its scene, renderer, exposure, shadow settings, and render loop. Existing meshes need light-responsive materials; the supplied pulse shader does not itself consume scene lights.

